<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eastern Chalukyas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">624–c. 1070</span> · Also known as Chalukyas of Vengi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> were a dynasty that ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> region of coastal Andhra, between the Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers, from 624 to about 1070. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a>, a brother of the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a>, they long fought the Rashtrakutas. Their later rulers became closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> by marriage, and the line merged with the Chola dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulottunga I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas.jpg" alt="Eastern Chalukyas" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravishankar1827 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Chalukya_Maximum_Extent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
